    "48ca768a-a8cc-5596-a246-2cd45f23ca39": {
      "type": "StorySegmentLayer",
      "name": "11. What, when, where and how in 160 characters",
      "visible": true,
      "parameters": {
        "zoom": 10.46,
        "extent": [
          8532638.97,
          3274212.69,
          8643958.46,
          3382011.46
        ],
        "enableIdentify": false,
        "content": {
          "contentMode": "markdown",
          "markdown": "*4 · Message design principles · Additional method*\n\n1. **What (value and category), when (IST peak), where (site), how (one or two actions)**. `short` for SMS/push, `long` for app detail and briefings.\n2. **CPCB category names verbatim** (Good / Satisfactory / Moderate / Poor / Very Poor / Severe), matching the news and the official app.\n3. **Name sensitive groups first** (children, older adults, pregnant women, respiratory patients).\n4. **GRAP as \"corresponding level\" only**; enforcement is CAQM's call, so \"may be enforced\" plus a pointer to official notices.\n5. **One sentence of meteorological reasoning**; people who understand why nights are worse comply better.\n6. **A fixed disclaimer**: \"AI-model (Aurora) guidance for information only; follow official CPCB/CAQM alerts.\"\n7. Hindi first, English second, Korean for the operating team. Templates live in `HEALTH_ADVICE`, `GRAP_ADVICE` and `CATEGORY_NAMES` so health experts can review wording without code.\n",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": ""
        },
        "transition": {
          "type": "smooth",
          "time": 2.0
        },
